\section{Class Hierarchies}
\begin{note}
When a class \lstinline{B} derives from a class \lstinline{A},
we write \lstinline{class B(A):}.
The function \lstinline{super()} returns the next class in the \emph{method resolution order} (MRO),
which for simple cases is just the parent class \lstinline{A}.
Because the parent class owns its own state,
its \lstinline{__init__} method is responsible for setting that state;
the child's \lstinline{__init__} should therefore call \lstinline{super().__init__(...)} to run it.
If the child forgets this call,
then any attributes that only the parent sets will not exist on the child.
\end{note}
